/-! # One affine section chart at the strict-local identity

Source: agent-formulated strict-local input for the fibrewise cubical ampleness argument,
recorded with Raynaud, Faisceaux amples sur les schemas en groupes, LNM 119 (1970).
The original Raynaud source is unavailable; no numbered assertion of it is claimed.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-cubical-strict-local-affine-seed)
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option relaxedAutoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory Limits
open scoped CategoryTheory.MonObj

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A single affine nonvanishing chart containing the closed identity after strict
henselization. The chosen henselization is presented as a colimit of the recorded etale
triples for the chosen separable closure, and rho is its canonical structural ring map.
The base changes of the supplied cubical line have their actual tensor-pullback modules.

Source: agent-formulated strict-local affine-chart input, recorded with Raynaud LNM 119;
no numbered assertion of the unavailable original is claimed.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-cubical-strict-local-affine-seed).

Proof, following the nine steps of the node:
1. We use the following elementary section arguments, on any quasi-compact scheme $Z$. If $E$ is
ample and $V$ is globally generated, choose at $z$ a section $t$ of $E^a$ with affine $Z_t$ and
a section $v$ of $V$ nonzero at $z$. Then $t\otimes v^a$ is a section of $(E\otimes V)^a$,
nonzero at $z$; its locus is $Z_t\cap Z_v$, affine by the affine-cap lemma. Hence $E\otimes V$
is ample. If $E,F$ are both ample, a positive power $F^b$ is globally generated by the coherent-
twist theorem applied to $\mathcal O_Z$; $E^b$ is ample by the positive-power theorem. The
preceding argument makes $(E\otimes F)^b$ ample. An ample positive power implies the line itself
ample directly from the definition, by interpreting sections of $(L^b)^a$ as sections of
$L^{ab}$. We use these section arguments below, including their last positive-power converse.
Dependencies: def-weil-pbc-ample-invertible-module, lem-sc-affine-cap-section-open, lem-weil-
pbc-coherent-twist-global-generation, lem-sc-ample-positive-tensor-power.
2. Put $S=\operatorname{Spec}A$ and let $\eta$ be its generic point. Normality of the local ring
makes $A$ an integrally closed domain, so $S$ is normal integral Noetherian affine. Semiabelian
geometric fibres are connected and nonempty: a clopen decomposition is constant on every coset
of the connected split torus, descends through its fppf quotient to the connected abelian
variety, and is constant; the identity gives nonemptiness. Apply the generic torus-quotient
theorem to get $q:H\to J$, a torsor under a torus $T/S$, with $J/S$ smooth separated commutative
of finite presentation, semiabelian fibres, and $J_\eta$ an abelian variety. The normal-smooth-
group local-ample theorem gives ample lines on $J$ over an affine open cover of $S$. Any member
containing the closed point of the local scheme $S$ is all of $S$, so obtain an ample line $M$
on $J$. Put $F=M\otimes[-1]^*M$. Inversion is an affine isomorphism, so its pullback of $M$ is
ample; step 1 makes $F$ ample, and tensor symmetry makes it symmetric.
Dependencies: def-weil-pbc-normal-ring, def-sfb-semiabelian-geometric-fibres, lem-sfb-normal-
semiabelian-generic-torus-quotient, lem-sfb-normal-smooth-group-local-ample-line, lem-weil-pbc-
pullback-ample-invertible, lem-weil-pbc-invertible-pullback-tensor.
3. The torus $T/S$ is affine: fppf locally it is a product of copies of $\mathbf G_m$, hence
affine, and affineness descends along fpqc coverings. Likewise $q$ is affine: it is an fpqc
cover, and its base change along itself is the projection $H\times_ST\to H$ by the torsor
identity, which is affine by base change from $T\to S$. Apply affine fpqc descent to $q$.
Consequently $q^*$ preserves ampleness by the ample affine-pullback theorem. The morphisms
$J_\eta\to J$ and $H_\eta\to H$ are affine too, since they are base changes of the morphism
between affine schemes $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa(\eta)\to S$. In particular $F_\eta$ is
symmetric ample.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-fpqc-descend-affine, lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence, lem-
weil-pbc-pullback-ample-invertible.
4. Write $N=B\otimes\iota^*B$. The assumed ampleness of $B_{(0)}=B_\eta$, inversion and step 1
make $N_\eta$ ample. Apply rational Picard surjectivity for the torus torsor $q_\eta:H_\eta\to
J_\eta$ to $B_\eta$. Its target is smooth, separated and finite type; it is geometrically
integral by the smooth-connected-fibre integrality theorem, since its geometric fibres are
semiabelian and hence connected as checked in step 2. Obtain $c\ge1$ and a line $D$ on $J_\eta$
with $q_\eta^*D\simeq B_\eta^c$. Set $C=D\otimes[-1]^*D$. This line is symmetric and, since $q$
commutes with inversion, $q_\eta^*C\simeq N_\eta^c$, which is ample. Apply the separate abelian-
quotient ample-reflection theorem to this semiabelian extension to deduce that $C$ itself is
ample on $J_\eta$. This reflection is specific to an extension of an abelian variety by a torus,
not a general fpqc descent of absolute ampleness.
Dependencies: lem-sfb-torus-torsor-picard-rational-surjectivity, lem-sfb-semiabelian-abelian-
quotient-ample-reflection, lem-weil-pbc-invertible-pullback-tensor, lem-sc-ample-positive-
tensor-power, lem-trf-smooth-connected-fibre-normal-integrality.
5. Apply coherent-twist global generation on $J_\eta$ with ample line $C$ and the finite-type
quasi-coherent line $F_\eta^{-1}$. Choose $d\ge2$ so that $F_\eta^{-1}\otimes C^{d-1}$ is
globally generated. By step 1, $E=C^d\otimes F_\eta^{-1}$ is ample, and it is symmetric. Apply
the symmetric-ample endomorphism-span theorem to $F_\eta$ and $E$ on the abelian variety
$J_\eta$. It gives $a\ge1$, $r\ge1$ and endomorphisms $b_1,\ldots,b_r$ with $E^a\simeq\bigotimes
b_j^*F_\eta$. Extend each $b_j$ uniquely to an endomorphism of $J/S$ by normal-semiabelian
generic homomorphism extension. Define the actual line $Q=F^a\otimes\bigotimes b_j^*F$ on all of
$J$. The displayed comparison gives $Q_\eta\simeq F_\eta^a\otimes E^a\simeq C^{da}$.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-coherent-twist-global-generation, lem-sfb-abelian-symmetric-ample-
endomorphism-span, lem-sfb-normal-semiabelian-hom-extension, lem-weil-pbc-invertible-pullback-
tensor.
6. Choose $v\ge1$ so that $F^v$ is generated by finitely many global sections, again by
coherent-twist global generation with $\mathcal O_J$. Their pullbacks generate every $b_j^*F^v$:
at a point a nonzero generating fibre value pulls back to a nonzero value after the residue-
field extension, by the invertible-pullback fibre formula. The tensor-globally-generated theorem
makes $V=\bigotimes b_j^*F^v$ globally generated (and supplies finite generators); $J$ is quasi-
compact, as a finitely presented scheme over affine $S$. The line $F^{av}$ is ample by the
positive-power theorem. Step 1 therefore makes $Q^v=F^{av}\otimes V$ ample and then makes $Q$
ample. Hence $q^*Q$ is ample on $H$ by step 3.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-coherent-twist-global-generation, lem-weil-pbc-invertible-pullback-
tensor, lem-sc-tensor-globally-generated, lem-sc-ample-positive-tensor-power, lem-weil-pbc-
pullback-ample-invertible, def-sbc-locally-finite-presentation.
7. With $h=cda>0$, step 4 and step 5 give $(q^*Q)_\eta\simeq N_\eta^h$. Thus
$V=N^h\otimes(q^*Q)^{-1}$ is generically trivial. Apply the normal-Noetherian generic-line-
pullback theorem to $p:H\to S$ and $V$. Both schemes are locally Noetherian: $S$ is Noetherian
and $H$ is finite type over $S$. Smoothness gives flatness and local finite presentation. All
residue fibres are integral and nonempty by the smooth-connected-fibre normal-integrality
theorem, using the connectedness checked in step 2. Finite presentation includes quasi-
compactness, so its last hypothesis holds as well. Obtain $V\simeq p^*W$ for an invertible
module $W$ on $S$. This module is trivial: a trivializing open containing the closed point of
$S$ is all of $S$. Consequently $N^h\simeq q^*Q$ is ample. The positive-power converse in step 1
gives that the supplied global norm $N$ is ample on $H$.
Dependencies: lem-sfb-normal-noetherian-generic-line-pullback, lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-
noetherian, lem-pc-smooth-flat, lem-trf-smooth-connected-fibre-normal-integrality, def-sbc-
locally-finite-presentation, def-sc-invertible-module.
8. Apply cubical ample-norm reflection over $S$ to the GIVEN $(B,r,\tau)$. Its base is normal
integral Noetherian affine and its group is smooth separated commutative of finite presentation
with semiabelian geometric fibres, as required. Step 7 verifies its crucial hypothesis, absolute
ampleness of $B\otimes\iota^*B$ on all of $H$, rather than only fibrewise ampleness. Its
conclusion makes $B$ ample on $H$. No use is made of the parent fibrewise-ample criterion, the
supplied-norm-ampleness theorem, or their descendants: those would be circular here.
Dependencies: lem-sfb-cubical-ample-norm-reflection.
9. The projection $g:X=H\times_A\operatorname{Spec}R\to H$ is affine, being the base change of a
morphism between affine schemes. Pullback of the ample $B$ along $g$ makes $L$ ample on $X$.
Apply the definition of ampleness at the point $e_R(\mathfrak m_R)$. It supplies $n\ge1$ and
$s\in\Gamma(X,L^{\otimes n})$ with affine $X_s$ containing that point. Membership in the
nonvanishing locus means exactly $s(e_R(\mathfrak m_R))\ne0$, as required. All residue-fibre
hypotheses of the original statement are retained; the argument uses in particular the generic
one.
Dependencies: lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence, lem-weil-pbc-pullback-ample-invertible,
def-weil-pbc-ample-invertible-module.
-/
theorem cubical_strict_local_affine_seed
    (A : CommRingCat.{u}) [IsLocalRing A] [IsNoetherianRing A]
    (hnormal : ∀ (p : Ideal A) [p.IsPrime],
      IsDomain (Localization.AtPrime p) ∧ IsIntegrallyClosed (Localization.AtPrime p))
    (K : Type u) [Field K]
    [Algebra ((Spec A).residueField (IsLocalRing.closedPoint A)) K]
    [IsSepClosure ((Spec A).residueField (IsLocalRing.closedPoint A)) K]
    (c : Cocone (Scheme.etaleResidueTriplesRingDiagram A (IsLocalRing.closedPoint A) K
      (CommRingCat.ofHom
        (algebraMap ((Spec A).residueField (IsLocalRing.closedPoint A)) K))))
    (hc : IsColimit c) [IsLocalRing c.pt]
    (ρ : A ⟶ c.pt) (hρ : ∀ t, t.α ≫ c.ι.app t = ρ)
    (H : Grp (Over (Spec A))) [IsCommMonObj H.X]
    [Smooth H.X.hom] [IsSeparated H.X.hom]
    [LocallyOfFinitePresentation H.X.hom] [QuasiCompact H.X.hom]
    (hsemiabelian : HasSemiabelianGeometricFibres H)
    (B : CubicalRigidifiedLine H.X)
    (hfibre : ∀ p : Spec A,
      @Scheme.Modules.IsAmple
        (CubicalLine.baseChangedGroup ((Spec A).fromSpecResidueField p) H.X).left
        (B.baseChange ((Spec A).fromSpecResidueField p)).line
        (B.baseChange ((Spec A).fromSpecResidueField p)).invertible) :
    ∃ n : ℕ, 0 < n ∧
      ∃ s : ((B.baseChange (Spec.map ρ)).line.tensorPow n).val.presheaf.obj (Opposite.op ⊤),
        ∃ U : (CubicalLine.baseChangedGroup (Spec.map ρ) H.X).left.Opens,
          IsAffineOpen U ∧
          (η[CubicalLine.baseChangedGroup (Spec.map ρ) H.X]).left
            (IsLocalRing.closedPoint c.pt) ∈ U ∧
          (U : Set (CubicalLine.baseChangedGroup (Spec.map ρ) H.X).left) =
            Scheme.Modules.nonvanishingLocus
              ((B.baseChange (Spec.map ρ)).line.tensorPow n) s := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
